# TRANSPORT: the shipped guard templates, executed against real host events.
#
# The sibling gate in internal/agent/guard_test.go checks that every host DECLARES a stance for
# every decision and input. A declaration can be sincere and still wrong, because
# nothing there runs the file: the OpenCode plugin declared a correct stance for
# months while invoking a subcommand that no longer existed, so it never received a
# verdict to have a stance about, and every session it guarded ran unguarded.
#
# These cases close that hole for the shipped templates. They run the real files
# (__MAGUS_TEMPLATES is an absolute path to docs/guides/integrations/agents, never a
# copy) against the event shapes their hosts actually send, and assert the response
# a host would receive. `magus` here is the CLI under test, resolved off PATH the
# same way the templates resolve it in a session.
#
# Every template is Buzz, run the way a host config wires it: a plain
# `magus buzz -s <file> [-- flags]` argv. The per-entry knobs ride that argv: the host
# name, and the `magus shell` flags an entry declares about itself. Whether to forward
# the whole event is worked out from the event.
#
# Every case is labeled `# case: <input>/<decision>`; TestTransportCasesCoverTheContract
# in internal/agent/guard_test.go fails when the guard contract grows a cell no case covers.

# The glue itself runs anywhere magus does; the fixtures do not. The stale binary below is
# a POSIX sh script, and the push cases write their run log with sh.
[windows] skip 'the fixtures stand in a POSIX sh script for a stale magus'

# The host every case below names on its argv unless it says otherwise. The name is
# opaque to magus; codex is the one the templates read.
env HOST_NAME=testscript

# The shared templates serve these two hosts with the same documented envelope
# fields and reply dialect. Keep the hosts explicit: TestTransportCasesCover-
# TheHostContract reads this marker and refuses a coverage claim that no executed
# fixture reaches. A shared fixture is intentional here; copying the identical
# JSON would create two opportunities for the recorded contract to drift.
# hosts: claude-code,codex

# --- magus-command.buzz: the shared Claude Code / Codex shape ----------

# case: command/deny
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout '"permissionDecisionReason":"whole-tree'
stdout 'whole-tree git stash'

# case: command/advise
stdin advise-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"'
! stdout 'permissionDecision'

# case: command/advise
# The suppressed shape, for a host that does worse than ignore a reply it does not
# know: one that treats an unsupported key as an error and then fails OPEN is
# disarmed by an advisory rather than merely deaf to it. No documented host needs
# this today: the one that did was suppressed on a reading its vendor's current
# reference contradicts, so the flag has no user and this case is what keeps it
# working for the host you wire. Suppressed, the advise must render NOTHING, not an
# empty envelope, which is still a key the host would parse.
env __MAGUS_NO_ADVISE=1
stdin advise-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/deny
# Suppression must not touch the deny arm, which is the whole point of splitting it.
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
env __MAGUS_NO_ADVISE=

# case: command/pass
# An allowed call must produce NOTHING. A host reads an empty response as allow, so
# a template that emitted an envelope here would turn every benign command into a
# decision the host has to interpret.
stdin pass-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/advise
# A PreToolUse shell command comes back rewritten to run with stdin at end-of-file: the
# whole tool_input returns (updatedInput replaces it), with the command prefixed
# `exec </dev/null; ` and every other field kept. No permissionDecision rides with it, so
# the host's own permission rules still judge the call. The first rewrite of a session
# says so, once.
stdin rewrite-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"updatedInput":'
stdout '"command":"exec \\u003c/dev/null; ls -la"'
stdout '"description":"list it"'
stdout '"timeout":60000'
stdout '"additionalContext":"magus runs your shell commands with stdin at end-of-file'
! stdout 'permissionDecision'

# case: command/pass
# A command that already closes stdin is never prefixed twice, so a pass is nothing again.
stdin rewrite-wrapped.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/deny
# A refused call is never rewritten.
stdin rewrite-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
! stdout 'updatedInput'

# case: command/pass
# Codex applies updatedInput only beside permissionDecision "allow", which would turn a
# pass into an approval, so it is never handed one.
stdin rewrite-command-codex.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name codex
! stdout .

# --- magus-command.buzz: a host's Read tool, restated as the line it stands for ---
#
# The Read tool never reaches a shell, so the glue restates it: a whole Read as a whole-file
# print (`cat`), a ranged one as a ranged print (`sed -n`). The read rules then judge it as
# the command it replaces. These run the Buzz glue alone, the form the wirings name; the
# fixture workspace sets read-navigation to deny past 120 lines and holds a Go file over
# that, so the whole print is refused and the ranged one is not.
cd read-ws

# case: command/deny
# No offset and no limit: the whole file.
stdin $WORK/read-whole.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'big.go'

# case: command/deny
# A limit past 300 lines counts as whole.
stdin $WORK/read-huge-limit.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'

# case: command/pass
# An offset and a limit inside the threshold is the bounded read the deny points to.
stdin $WORK/read-range.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout 'permissionDecision'
cd $WORK

# --- magus-command.buzz: MCP tool calls, forwarded whole -------------------

# Both MCP cases run under a BOUND lease, in the one fixture workspace this archive
# carries, because the rule that reads an MCP call is lease-scoped. The row is declared
# first: an id the ledger does not carry is refused by a different rule, and the case would
# then pass for a reason that has nothing to do with this transport. The guard binds whoever
# it lets `magus job exec` through, keyed on the session the event names, so the exec goes
# through the template as the session both MCP events carry.
#
# Declaring a row reaches the user-global state dir, which the archive's read-only HOME is
# not: both are pointed at the work directory for the length of this block.
mkdir $WORK/home
env HOME=$WORK/home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=$WORK/home/state
cd notes-ws
exec magus job fork harness/mcp-worker --read-only --criteria 'the transport test set, held so the rebind rule has a job to read'
stdin $WORK/exec-mcp-worker.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME

# Both MCP cases forward the WHOLE event, which the glue works out from the event: an MCP
# call carries no tool_input.command for HOST_EVENT_PATH to select. A derivation that
# regressed to selecting `null` would judge text instead of a tool call, and the deny below
# would vanish.

# case: mcp/pass
# A judged MCP call that no rule refuses. `magus\describe` reads, and a bound worker
# reading its own workspace is the ordinary case, so this must render NOTHING. Paired with
# the deny below it pins both directions of the raw arm: that the envelope reaches the
# rules at all, and that arriving there is not itself a refusal.
stdin $WORK/mcp-read.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: mcp/deny
# A client script calling magus\job.clear() would drop every job, which is the one thing a
# bound worker may never do, so leaseRebind refuses it and the same hookSpecificOutput reply
# this file renders for shell commands carries the deny here. The job door is the
# client tool's script: magus serves no per-verb job tool, so a call naming one is not a
# call to magus at all.
stdin $WORK/mcp-call.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'would drop every job'
cd $WORK
env HOME=/no-home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=
# The binding is dropped here, not left for the cases below: every path case in this
# fixture workspace would otherwise be graded against write paths nobody declared for it, and
# the lease rules answer before the rule each of those cases is about. The session's binding
# is filed beside the job store, so the state dir goes with it.
rm $WORK/home/state

# --- the edges of the whole-event derivation --------------------------------
#
# The glue works out which payload to send from the event, so these are the shapes where
# working it out could go wrong. Each says which branch it must take, and the last one is
# the one that would be SILENT: a params object carrying a `command` key, where selecting
# the field judges a string that was never a command line.

# case: command/pass
# An empty command is still a STRING, so it takes the field branch and magus judges an
# empty command line. It must not be read as an absent field and turned into an envelope.
stdin $WORK/empty-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/pass
# Present but NULL. The glue cannot tell a JSON null from an absent key and sends the
# envelope, where the decoder finds no command, no path and no prompt and says there is
# nothing to judge.
stdin $WORK/null-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/pass
# No command field at all, and no other shape magus reads either. The envelope goes whole
# and the decoder answers that it holds nothing to judge.
stdin $WORK/no-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/pass
# The silent one. `magus shell` reads an MCP call off the TOOL NAME, ahead of any `command`
# in its params, so this whole event has to reach it: reduced to its `command` key it would
# be judged as a shell line and DENIED on a rule about `git stash`, which is a refusal of a
# read-only describe call for words in a parameter. The glue recognizes the MCP namespace.
stdin $WORK/mcp-command-param.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# --- the per-entry flag route: the argv after `--` ---------------------------
#
# A spawn carries a prompt and a subagent type rather than one string, so it takes the same
# whole-event route the MCP cases above do. What it adds is the OTHER knob: the capability
# an entry declares about itself. Only a wiring that also matches its host's skill tool may
# pass --reports-skills, so the rule that requires a skill before a spawn fires
# exactly where loads are reported.

# Inside a fixture workspace, because the rule stands down without a cache dir to key its
# per-session marker on: that is the same fail-open the flag itself exists for, and a case
# run at $WORK would pass both ways and pin nothing. The workspace sets spawn-unbriefed to
# deny, since it only advises by default.
cd push-ws

# What varies between the two cases below is only the flag.

# case: command/pass
# Unflagged: the wiring reports no skill loads, so the rule stands down rather than denying
# every spawn on a host that cannot satisfy it.
stdin $WORK/spawn-call.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: command/deny
# A SUPPORTED flag: the glue matches it against the set it publishes and passes it on.
stdin $WORK/spawn-call.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'magus-multi-agent'

# case: command/deny
# An argument the glue does not know. Forwarded, `magus shell` would reject it with a usage
# error, losing the verdict. The glue refuses the argument, says which one on stderr, and
# judges the call anyway. A misconfigured entry must not block work, and must not be silent: both halves are
# asserted below, because either one alone is a shape somebody would settle for.
stdin $WORK/deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name testscript --not-a-flag
stderr 'magus-command.buzz: unsupported argument "--not-a-flag"'
stderr 'judged WITHOUT it'
stderr 'Supported: --reports-skills'
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'whole-tree git stash'

# case: command/deny
# An EMPTY argument, which a host config produces by expanding a variable that is unset.
# The remedy line is the only one a misconfigured reader gets, and unquoted it read
# "unsupported argument ;" with no subject at all.
stdin $WORK/deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name testscript ''
stderr 'magus-command.buzz: unsupported argument ""'
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'

# case: command/deny
# A supported flag given twice, which is what two hook entries merged by hand look like.
# Forwarded twice it reached `magus shell` as a repeated flag; deduped, the verdict is the
# one the single-flag case above already pins.
stdin $WORK/deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name testscript --reports-skills --reports-skills
! stderr 'unsupported argument'
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'

cd $WORK

# --- magus-command.buzz: a message the person typed ---------------------------
#
# An event that names no tool and carries text at `prompt` (Claude Code's envelope key) is
# the person's message, not a call. The glue hands it to `magus shell --message` and says
# NOTHING: a message hook's stdout reaches the model, and a recorded topic is not news to it. What the record does
# shows on the next call, which architecture-unbriefed holds until the agent loads the
# architecture skill. The workspace sets that rule to deny, since it only advises by
# default.

cd arch-ws

stdin $WORK/prompt-architecture.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stdout .
! stderr .

# case: command/deny
stdin $WORK/arch-bash.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'magus-architecture-review'

# case: command/pass
# Loading the skill clears it.
stdin $WORK/arch-skill.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stdout .
stdin $WORK/arch-bash.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stdout .

# case: command/pass
# A message that asks no structure question records nothing, so that session's next call runs.
stdin $WORK/prompt-ordinary.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stdout .
! stderr .
stdin $WORK/arch-bash-s2.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stdout .

# case: path/deny
# The path entry declares the capability too, and a write that creates a directory waits
# on the same load whatever was asked.
stdin $WORK/arch-new-dir.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stderr 'unsupported argument'
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'creating the new directory'

# case: path/advise
# Unflagged, the rule stands down and the new-directory advisory speaks instead.
stdin $WORK/arch-new-dir.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'additionalContext'
stdout 'NEW DIRECTORY'

cd $WORK

# case: mcp/advise unreachable - no rule ADVISES on an MCP call. Every rule that reads one
# denies (the lease-scoped gate, VCS and rebind rules, and the cache-dir rule), and the
# three advisory families a rendered call could reach each need machine state a script
# cannot make deterministic: the gate-repeat count, the stale symbol index, the stale
# binary. A case here would pass for a reason that is not this transport.

# --- magus-path.buzz: the same hosts, their file-writing tool -----------

# case: path/advise
stdin advise-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"'
stdout 'every session'

# case: path/advise
# The same suppression on the other input, so a host that needs the flag gets it
# on both rather than half-silenced.
env __MAGUS_NO_ADVISE=1
stdin advise-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
env __MAGUS_NO_ADVISE=

# case: path/advise
# A host tool that names its target something other than file_path. magus reads every
# `*_path` spelling off the ENVELOPE, so this only reaches a rule when the whole event is
# forwarded; selecting one dot-path handed the guard the literal "null" and every write
# rule went unrun, on a matcher both shipped configs wire.
stdin notebook-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"'
stdout 'every session'

# case: path/deny
# An envelope that arrives truncated. Judged as text it matches no rule and passes, which
# is the one silent failure for file writes: the intact event advises, and a byte short of
# it said nothing at all.
stdin truncated-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'nothing was judged'

# case: path/pass
stdin pass-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# case: path/deny
# The notes store is the one rule that denies on file writes, and it fires only where a
# workspace DECLARES one - so this runs in a subdirectory that does, leaving every other
# case judged against a tree with no notes store at all.
#
# This case is why the deny arm exists. Before it, HOST_RESPONSE rendered only `advise`,
# so a deny produced EMPTY stdout while magus exited 2, and the glue read
# empty-plus-nonzero as a broken guard and exited 0, which every host takes as allow. The
# rule looked enforced and blocked nothing.
cd notes-ws
stdin $WORK/deny-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'NOTES store'
stdout 'magus notes edit'
cd $WORK

# --- cursor-hook.buzz: one file, wired events, a different response dialect ---
#
# Cursor splits a verdict across two events: a gating event carries a deny and no
# message on an allow, so the advisory has to ride the post-tool event. These cases
# run both halves, because the declaration now claims advise=model on both inputs
# and only the file can show whether the text really arrives.
# hosts: cursor

# case: command/deny
stdin cursor-shell-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"deny"'
stdout '"agent_message":"whole-tree'
stdout 'whole-tree git stash'

# case: command/pass
# Cursor is the one host that must answer on an allow rather than stay silent.
stdin cursor-shell-pass.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'

# case: command/deny
# The same call under an entry that names no host is refused on the gating event rather
# than allowed, and says why on both channels (MGS3024).
stdin cursor-shell-pass.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz
stdout '"permission":"deny"'
stdout 'MGS3024'
stderr 'MGS3024'

# case: command/advise
# The gating event allows and says nothing, which is all it can do; the same call's
# postToolUse is what carries the explanation.
stdin cursor-shell-advise.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'
! stdout 'additional_context'

stdin cursor-post-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"additional_context":"'

# A postToolUse on a command the rules pass renders a reply Cursor can parse and
# nothing else. An empty stdout would be a second thing for the host to interpret.
stdin cursor-post-command-pass.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '\{\}'
! stdout 'additional_context'

# case: path/deny
# preToolUse gates the write. This is what moving off afterFileEdit bought: the
# verdict arrives before the file changes, so a deny is a block rather than a report.
cd notes-ws
stdin $WORK/cursor-pre-write-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"deny"'
stdout 'NOTES store'
cd $WORK

# case: path/pass
stdin cursor-pre-write-pass.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'

# case: path/advise
# The declared-output rule advises rather than denies, so the gate lets it through
# and postToolUse delivers the explanation to the model.
stdin cursor-pre-write-advise.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'

stdin cursor-post-write.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"additional_context":"'
stdout 'every session'

# --- cursor-hook.buzz: MCP via beforeMCPExecution ---------------------------
#
# The coverage marker now claims mcp deny/ask for Cursor. These cases run the
# real beforeMCPExecution envelope (mcp_server_name + tool_name + tool_input)
# so a declaration that no fixture reaches cannot land again. Same lease
# scaffold as the shared Claude/Codex MCP block: the rebind rule is lease-
# scoped, and an unbound session would refuse for a different reason.

mkdir $WORK/home
env HOME=$WORK/home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=$WORK/home/state
cd notes-ws
exec magus job fork harness/cursor-mcp-worker --read-only --criteria 'the cursor MCP transport cases, held so the rebind rule has a job to read'
stdin $WORK/cursor-exec-mcp-worker.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor

# case: mcp/pass
# A judged MCP call that no rule refuses. Cursor answers allow on the gate
# (unlike the silent shared templates), so the reply must name allow.
stdin $WORK/cursor-mcp-read.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'

# case: mcp/deny
# job.clear from a bound worker is lease-rebind refuse; Cursor's dialect carries
# permission=deny on both channels.
stdin $WORK/cursor-mcp-call.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"deny"'
stdout 'would drop every job'
cd $WORK
env HOME=/no-home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=
rm $WORK/home/state

# A preToolUse on a tool carrying neither field allows without asking magus
# anything. Cursor's preToolUse fires for every tool, and the shell tool in
# particular is beforeShellExecution's to judge, and twice would record two verdicts.
stdin cursor-pre-other.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'

# sessionEnd records where the work stopped and says nothing at all. A checkpoint
# is not a verdict, so any reply here would be one the host has to interpret.
stdin cursor-session-end.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
! stdout .

# subagentStart records the handed work and always allows. A lease prompt is prose,
# so nothing judges it; the reshape is what makes Cursor's `task` field legible as
# the spawn magus records.
stdin cursor-subagent.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'
! stdout 'deny'

# --- magus-observe.buzz: the verdict-free one ----------------------------
#
# It declares no guard coverage, so the contract gate above owes it no case and
# would never have asked for one. That exemption is exactly how an untested
# artifact ships: the OpenCode plugin in the header note declared a correct stance
# and was still broken, because nothing ran it. These cases run it.
#
# There is no `# case: <input>/<decision>` label on purpose. An observation is
# not a verdict on an input, and labeling it would make TestTransportCasesCover-
# TheContract count a cell the guard contract does not have.

# It records a read and says NOTHING to the host. A read is not a decision, so any
# stdout here would be a response the host has to interpret on every file it opens.
stdin observe-read.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-observe.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

# A payload it cannot parse is dropped silently, and exits 0: some hosts read a non-zero
# pre-tool hook as "block this call", which would stop the agent reading anything.
stdin observe-garbage.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-observe.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

# A search event names no read path, so there is nothing to record and nothing to
# say. Recording the event whole here would put the query text in the trail.
stdin observe-search.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-observe.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

# An entry that names no host records nothing and says why, coded.
stdin observe-read.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-observe.buzz
! stdout .
stderr 'MGS3024'

# --- the two lifecycle wrappers: a stop record and a post-compaction brief ----
#
# They carry no verdict either, so the contract gate owes them no case and, until this
# block, none existed: the Stop and SessionStart hooks this repository wires were the
# only shipped glue nothing ever ran. That is precisely the exemption the OpenCode
# plugin in the header note shipped behind.
#
# No `# case: <input>/<decision>` label, for the reason the observe block carries
# none: a checkpoint and a brief are not verdicts on an input.
#
# Both write to the user-global state dir, which the archive's read-only HOME is not.
env HOME=$WORK/home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=$WORK/home/state
cd notes-ws

# The Stop wrapper forwards the event whole and says NOTHING. Anything on either stream
# would reach the host as this hook's reply at the end of every session.
stdin $WORK/stop-event.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-checkpoint.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

# An envelope it cannot parse is still silent, and still exits 0. This wrapper selects
# nothing out of the event, so a malformed one is magus's to ignore rather than the
# wrapper's to choke on - and a Stop hook that exits non-zero is a session-ending error
# notice in every host that shows one.
stdin $WORK/observe-garbage.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-checkpoint.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

# An entry that names no host records no checkpoint and says why, coded.
stdin $WORK/stop-event.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-checkpoint.buzz
! stdout .
stderr 'MGS3024'

# It reached magus rather than being silent because it resolved nothing: the checkpoints
# just recorded are in the session the wrapper named.
exec magus session
stdout 'testscript'

# The brief cases move to a workspace nothing above has touched, with a state dir of
# their own, so the brief reads a checkout no guard case above left a trail in.
cd $WORK/brief-ws
env HOME=$WORK/brief-home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=$WORK/brief-home/state

# The SessionStart wrapper reads no event at all and hands back the brief. The rules line
# is the one host-shaped part, and it prints only because this fixture carries the file.
stdin $WORK/session-start.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-rehydrate.buzz
stdout 'standing rules: CLAUDE.md'
! stderr .

# The json channel, for a host that parses stdout as a reply rather than appending it.
# The envelope is asserted whole: a host that got the text without the wrapper would drop
# it silently, which looks exactly like a hook that never fired. The brief's own line
# breaks arrive as the two characters a JSON string spells one with.
stdin $WORK/session-start.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-rehydrate.buzz -- --format json
stdout '^\{"hookSpecificOutput":\{"hookEventName":"SessionStart","additionalContext":"magus brief: '
stdout 'standing rules: CLAUDE.md; re-read it, the summary above is not it\\n"\}\}$'
! stderr .

# A host naming an instruction file this checkout does not carry gets the brief without
# the line, rather than a sentence pointing at nothing.
stdin $WORK/session-start.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-rehydrate.buzz -- --rules NOT-HERE.md
stdout 'magus brief: '
! stdout 'standing rules'

# Both flags at once, as the codex wiring passes them.
stdin $WORK/session-start.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-rehydrate.buzz -- --rules CLAUDE.md --format json
stdout '^\{"hookSpecificOutput":'
stdout 'standing rules: CLAUDE.md; re-read it'
! stderr .

# A flag this template does not declare is reported, and the brief still arrives.
stdin $WORK/session-start.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-rehydrate.buzz -- --agent-name testscript
stderr 'magus-rehydrate.buzz: unsupported argument "--agent-name"'
stdout 'magus brief: '
cd $WORK
env HOME=/no-home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=

# --- fail-open: a missing magus must degrade the way each template promises ---

env __MAGUS_BIN=$WORK/nonexistent-magus

# The observer stays silent and exits 0 when magus is absent. Unlike a guard, it
# enforces nothing, so an announcement per read would report only that an optional
# record was skipped.
stdin observe-read.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-observe.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

# A message is as silent: the guard's unavailable notice would reach the model as context
# on every turn, and nothing about the message itself was ever going to be enforced.
stdin $WORK/prompt-architecture.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME --reports-skills
! stdout .
! stderr .

# The two lifecycle wrappers degrade the same way and for the same reason: neither
# enforces anything, so a notice would interrupt the end of every session, or open every
# compacted one, to report that an optional record was not written.
stdin $WORK/stop-event.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-checkpoint.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
! stderr .

stdin $WORK/session-start.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-rehydrate.buzz
! stdout .
! stderr .

# The command template announces itself. An unguarded session you know about beats
# one you do not.
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'magus guard is NOT running'

# The path template stays silent by default: for most hosts an empty response means
# allow. That is the deliberate trade - this template can now DENY, so failing open here
# means an unavailable guard lets a notes write through, and announcing it on every file
# edit was judged the worse noise. __MAGUS_FAILED_RESPONSE is the opt-in for a host that
# would rather hear about it.
stdin advise-path.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-path.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# Cursor allows explicitly, because Cursor fails open on a hook crash anyway and
# pretending otherwise would give false assurance.
stdin cursor-shell-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'

# --- found, but cannot judge: the notice carries its own evidence -------------
#
# The case that actually occurs - a stale binary on PATH outlives a missing one - and
# the one whose notice used to name two suspects while checking neither. One of them,
# "cannot load this workspace", is not even a cause: the deny rules need no workspace.
# So these assert the three facts that END the search, and that the guess is gone.

env __MAGUS_BIN=$WORK/stale-magus
chmod 0755 $WORK/stale-magus

# One notice directory per case below, because the notice is now held to one firing per
# session and every fixture here carries the same session id. Sharing one directory would
# make each case assert on whether the case above it had already spoken.
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-stale-command
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'magus guard is NOT running'
stdout 'stale-magus'
stdout 'v0.0.1-stale'
stdout 'unknown subcommand'
! stdout 'cannot load this workspace'
# The config warning a too-old binary prints BEFORE it fails is a symptom of the same
# staleness. Reporting it as the error would hand back the least useful of the two lines.
! stdout 'WARN'

# Cursor's template is self-contained, so it carries the same evidence or none at all.
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-stale-cursor
stdin cursor-shell-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"allow"'
stderr 'stale-magus'
stderr 'v0.0.1-stale'
stderr 'unknown subcommand'

# A host that set its own response still gets exactly that: the evidence is the DEFAULT,
# not something the template forces on a host that made another choice.
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-stale-host
env __MAGUS_FAILED_RESPONSE=host-chose-this
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'host-chose-this'
! stdout 'stale-magus'
env __MAGUS_FAILED_RESPONSE=

# --- the notice is said once per session -------------------------------------
#
# These notices report a broken installation. That is a fact for the person and carries
# nothing an agent can act on, so a repeat is noise: measured at 2,741 unavailable and 653
# could-not-judge firings over recent sessions, 99% of them same-session repeats.

# A second call in the SAME session says nothing at all, and the verdict still fails open.
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-once
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'magus guard is NOT running'
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .

# A DIFFERENT session is owed the fact once, or the first session of the day would
# silence every session after it.
stdin deny-command-s2.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'magus guard is NOT running'

# The families are independent: a spent could-not-judge marker must not silence the
# missing-binary notice, which reports a different broken installation.
env __MAGUS_BIN=$WORK/does-not-exist
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout 'magus is not on PATH'
stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout .
env __MAGUS_BIN=$WORK/stale-magus

# Cursor holds its own notices the same way, on the stderr channel it uses.
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-once-cursor
stdin cursor-shell-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stderr 'magus guard is NOT running'
stdin cursor-shell-deny.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
! stderr 'magus guard is NOT running'

# --- a DENY never goes quiet, whatever any marker says -----------------------
#
# The safety property under the fire-once rules: an advisory may degrade because the
# reader already declined it, but a refusal is the one verdict the caller cannot see
# past, so it carries its whole reason every time. Asserted after the advisory family
# for this session has already been spent, which is the state a delegated worker
# arrives in - a Claude Code subagent shares its orchestrator's session id.
env __MAGUS_BIN=
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-deny

stdin advise-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"'

stdin advise-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"'

stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'whole-tree git stash'

stdin deny-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'whole-tree git stash'

# --- the push gate: consent comes from the person, through the host's prompt --
#
# An ungated push from a session no job lease binds gets the verdict ask. These cases run
# in a workspace with a commit and a run log that holds no gate at it, so the gate state
# is PROVEN absent: with no run log the rule stands down, and every push case above passes.
# Last in the file because the skip below would otherwise take every case after it along.
[!exec:git] skip 'the push cases need a git commit for the gate to be judged against'
env TMPDIR=$WORK/n-push
cd $WORK/push-ws
exec git init -q
exec git -c user.email=t@example.com -c user.name=T add magusfile.buzz
exec git -c user.email=t@example.com -c user.name=T commit -q -m init
mkdir .magus/runs

# case: path/ask unreachable - the push gate is the only rule that asks, and it reads a shell
# command; no rule asks about a write. The rendered arm is graded in internal/agent/guard_test.go.
# case: mcp/ask unreachable - the same: no rule asks about an MCP call.

# hosts: claude-code

# case: command/ask
stdin $WORK/push-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"ask"'
stdout 'Approving publishes'

# case: command/deny
# A caller that does not claim --renders-ask never receives an ask. A hook installed before
# the decision existed renders one as nothing, and its host reads nothing as allow, so magus
# refuses the push itself and says how to get the prompt back.
stdin $WORK/push-line.txt
! exec magus shell -o json
stdout '"decision": "deny"'
stdout 'predates approval prompts'
stdout 'magus describe harness'
! stdout '"decision": "ask"'

# case: command/deny
# The same holds for a template whose reply the reader wrote: only a reply the template
# assembled claims the flag.
env HOST_RESPONSE='{{if eq .decision "deny"}}{"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"deny","permissionDecisionReason":{{toJson .reason}}}}{{end}}'
stdin $WORK/push-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'predates approval prompts'
env HOST_RESPONSE=

# hosts: codex
env HOST_NAME=codex

# case: command/ask
# Codex parses a hook ask, marks the hook failed and runs the call, so this file never
# sends it. With no prompt rule in the project nothing would ask the person either: the
# push is refused, naming their own terminal.
stdin $WORK/push-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'own terminal'
stdout 'magus describe harness codex'
! stdout '"permissionDecision":"ask"'

mkdir .codex/rules
cp $WORK/push-rules.rules .codex/rules/magus.rules

# case: command/ask
# The rule prompts, so PreToolUse lets the call reach it and hands the model the reason.
stdin $WORK/push-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"pushing'
! stdout 'permissionDecision'

# case: command/ask
# A permission mode that never prompts would skip the rule, so the push is refused.
stdin $WORK/push-command-bypass.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'never prompts'
! stdout '"permissionDecision":"ask"'

# case: command/ask
# A push no prefix rule matches would run unprompted, so it is refused too.
stdin $WORK/push-command-dir.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'only a plain git push'

# case: command/ask
# The approval request Codex raises before its prompt gets no decision: the person decides.
stdin $WORK/push-approval.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '^\{"hookSpecificOutput":\{"hookEventName":"PermissionRequest"\}\}$'

# case: command/ask
# The payload never overrides the name. The same Codex-shaped event, turn_id and all, under
# an entry that names a different host gets THAT host's arm: the Claude Code ask. The host
# is what the configuration says, never what the event looks like.
env HOST_NAME=testscript
stdin $WORK/push-command-turn.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"ask"'
! stdout '"additionalContext":"pushing'

# case: command/deny
# An entry that names no host is refused with MGS3024, never defaulted to one host and never
# inferred from the event.
stdin $WORK/push-command-turn.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'MGS3024'
stdout 'magus describe harness'
! stdout '"permissionDecision":"ask"'
env HOST_NAME=codex

# case: command/pass
# A request magus did not configure is never approved on the guard's say-so.
stdin $WORK/other-approval.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '^\{"hookSpecificOutput":\{"hookEventName":"PermissionRequest"\}\}$'
! stdout 'allow'

# case: command/pass
# A push a passing gate covers is approved, so nobody is asked.
exec sh -c 'printf "{\"ts\":1,\"inv\":\"g\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"command\":{\"arguments\":[\"affected\",\"ci\"]},\"magus_version\":\"v0.0.0-1-g%s\"}\n{\"ts\":2,\"inv\":\"g\",\"kind\":\"finished\",\"status\":\"pass\"}\n" "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" > .magus/runs/gate.jsonl'
stdin $WORK/push-approval.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"decision":\{"behavior":"allow"\}'
stdin $WORK/push-command.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
! stdout 'permissionDecision'

# case: command/pass
# Every backend magus drives publishes through its own verb, and each plain push a gate
# covers is approved the same way.
stdin $WORK/push-approval-hg.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"decision":\{"behavior":"allow"\}'
stdin $WORK/push-approval-sl.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"decision":\{"behavior":"allow"\}'
stdin $WORK/push-approval-jj.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"decision":\{"behavior":"allow"\}'
rm .magus/runs/gate.jsonl

# case: command/ask
# An ungated push on each of them reaches its own prompt rule, so PreToolUse lets it through
# to that prompt rather than refusing it.
stdin $WORK/push-command-hg.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"pushing'
! stdout 'permissionDecision'
stdin $WORK/push-command-sl.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"pushing'
! stdout 'permissionDecision'
stdin $WORK/push-command-jj.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"additionalContext":"pushing'
! stdout 'permissionDecision'

# case: command/deny
# A session bound to a job forked under another is a worker, and workers do not publish:
# lease-vcs refuses the push outright, before the push gate grades it. A parentless job is
# the root's own, and its push is asked instead.
# The guard binds each session that runs `magus job exec` through it, so every worker below
# takes the job through its own template first.
env HOME=$WORK/home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=$WORK/home/state
exec magus job fork harness/push-worker --parent harness --read-only --criteria 'the push transport cases, held so the push gate has a worker to refuse'
stdin $WORK/exec-push-worker.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdin $WORK/push-approval.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"behavior":"deny"'
stdout 'stays with the orchestrator, not a worker'

# hosts: claude-code
env HOST_NAME=testscript

# case: command/deny
# A leased worker runs as its own session, so this refusal is its first and prints its
# verdict rather than the repeat line, whatever the orchestrator's session was told above.
stdin $WORK/exec-push-worker-s-worker.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdin $WORK/push-command-worker.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/magus-command.buzz -- --agent-name $HOST_NAME
stdout '"permissionDecision":"deny"'
stdout 'stays with the orchestrator, not a worker'

# hosts: cursor

# case: command/deny
stdin $WORK/cursor-exec-push-worker.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdin $WORK/cursor-push.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"deny"'
stdout 'stays with the orchestrator, not a worker'
rm $WORK/home/state
env HOME=/no-home
env XDG_STATE_HOME=

# case: command/ask
stdin $WORK/cursor-push.json
exec magus buzz -s $__MAGUS_TEMPLATES/cursor-hook.buzz -- --agent-name cursor
stdout '"permission":"ask"'
stdout 'Approving publishes'
cd $WORK

-- push-ws/magusfile.buzz --
import "magus";

magus\project({
    "sources": ["*.buzz"],
    "no_language": "fixture workspace: only the guard's view of an ungated push matters here",
});
magus\guard.builtins({"spawn-unbriefed": "deny"});
-- arch-ws/magusfile.buzz --
import "magus";

magus\project({
    "sources": ["*.buzz"],
    "no_language": "fixture workspace: only the guard's view of a message and the call after it matters here",
});
magus\guard.builtins({"architecture-unbriefed": "deny"});
-- prompt-architecture.json --
{"session_id": "arch-1", "hook_event_name": "UserPromptSubmit", "prompt": "does this respect our current architecture patterns?"}
-- prompt-ordinary.json --
{"session_id": "arch-2", "hook_event_name": "UserPromptSubmit", "prompt": "fix the flaky cache test"}
-- arch-bash.json --
{"session_id": "arch-1", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "ls -la"}}
-- arch-bash-s2.json --
{"session_id": "arch-2", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "ls -la"}}
-- arch-new-dir.json --
{"session_id": "arch-3", "tool_name": "Write", "tool_input": {"file_path": "newpkg/new.go", "content": "package newpkg\n"}}
-- arch-skill.json --
{"session_id": "arch-1", "tool_name": "Skill", "tool_input": {"skill": "magus-architecture-review"}}
-- push-rules.rules --
prefix_rule(
    pattern = ["git", "push"],
    decision = "prompt",
)
prefix_rule(
    pattern = ["hg", "push"],
    decision = "prompt",
)
prefix_rule(
    pattern = ["sl", "push"],
    decision = "prompt",
)
prefix_rule(
    pattern = ["jj", "git", "push"],
    decision = "prompt",
)
-- push-approval-hg.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PermissionRequest", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "hg push -r ."}}
-- push-approval-sl.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PermissionRequest", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "sl push --to main"}}
-- push-approval-jj.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PermissionRequest", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "jj git push -b main"}}
-- push-command-hg.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "hg push"}}
-- push-command-sl.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "sl push --to main"}}
-- push-command-jj.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "jj git push"}}
-- push-command.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "git push origin HEAD"}}
-- push-command-worker.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s-worker", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "git push origin HEAD"}}
-- exec-push-worker.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s1", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "magus job exec harness/push-worker"}}
-- exec-push-worker-s-worker.json --
{"hook_event_name": "PreToolUse", "session_id": "s-worker", "permission_mode": "default", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "magus job exec harness/push-worker"}}
-- cursor-exec-push-worker.json --
{"hook_event_name": "beforeShellExecution", "conversation_id": "c1", "command": "magus job exec harness/push-worker", "cwd": ".", "sandbox": false}
-- push-line.txt --
